1
00:00:00,600 --> 00:00:04,153
If you've ever started a budget in January and quit by February,

2
00:00:04,313 --> 00:00:05,762
it probably wasn't your fault.

3
00:00:06,182 --> 00:00:08,974
Most budgets are built for a life that doesn't exist.

4
00:00:09,394 --> 00:00:11,214
One where you get paid once a month,

5
00:00:11,374 --> 00:00:12,629
every bill is the same,

6
00:00:12,789 --> 00:00:14,786
and nothing surprising ever happens.

7
00:00:15,556 --> 00:00:23,457
So today I'll show you the paycheck method. A budget built around when your money actually arrives, using real numbers, start to finish.

8
00:00:24,227 --> 00:00:26,997
First, why budgets break. There are three reasons.

9
00:00:27,417 --> 00:00:31,292
Reason one. You budget by the month, but you get paid every two weeks.

10
00:00:31,712 --> 00:00:34,953
Rent is due on the first. Your paycheck lands on the fifth.

11
00:00:35,373 --> 00:00:41,045
So for a few days, a perfectly good monthly budget says you're fine, and your bank account says you're not.

12
00:00:41,465 --> 00:00:50,111
Reason two. The surprise expenses aren't really surprises. Gifts, car registration, that subscription that renews every January.

13
00:00:50,531 --> 00:00:53,453
They happen every single year. We just don't plan for them.

14
00:00:53,873 --> 00:01:01,925
Reason three. The budget has no room to breathe. Cut every dollar of fun, and the first coffee you buy makes the whole thing feel broken.

15
00:01:02,345 --> 00:01:03,701
So let's fix all three.

16
00:01:04,121 --> 00:01:09,270
Here's our example. Take-home pay is eighteen hundred and fifty dollars, every two weeks.

17
00:01:10,040 --> 00:01:15,751
Step one. List every bill with its due date, and give each bill to the paycheck that arrives before it.

18
00:01:16,171 --> 00:01:22,877
Paycheck 1 covers rent, phone and the student loan: $1,585, leaving just $265.

19
00:01:23,297 --> 00:01:31,642
Paycheck 2 covers the car, internet, insurance, utilities and the card minimum: $786, leaving $1,064.

20
00:01:32,062 --> 00:01:38,583
See the problem? Half the month feels broke, and the other half feels rich. That's exactly when overspending happens.

21
00:01:39,003 --> 00:01:45,028
The fix: move $400 from paycheck 2 into a buffer that pays part of next month's rent.

22
00:01:45,448 --> 00:01:50,913
Now both paychecks have about $665 left over.

23
00:01:51,683 --> 00:01:54,010
Step two. Turn surprises into a bill.

24
00:01:54,430 --> 00:01:56,773
Add up every irregular cost from last year.

25
00:01:56,933 --> 00:01:58,927
Holidays and gifts, $600.

26
00:01:59,087 --> 00:02:00,638
Car repairs, $500.

27
00:02:00,798 --> 00:02:02,553
Medical copays, $300.

28
00:02:02,713 --> 00:02:04,405
Registration, $180.

29
00:02:04,565 --> 00:02:06,498
Annual subscriptions, $120.

30
00:02:06,918 --> 00:02:08,756
That's $1,700 a year.

31
00:02:09,176 --> 00:02:13,081
Divide by 26 paychecks: $66 per check.

32
00:02:13,241 --> 00:02:16,220
moved to a separate savings account the day you get paid.

33
00:02:16,640 --> 00:02:19,262
Next January, the gifts are already paid for.

34
00:02:20,032 --> 00:02:21,841
Step three. Pick one fun number.

35
00:02:22,261 --> 00:02:24,974
Groceries and gas get $320 per paycheck.

36
00:02:25,394 --> 00:02:28,964
Then $100 of fun money. No tracking, no guilt.

37
00:02:29,384 --> 00:02:33,589
When it's gone, it's gone. But spending it isn't failing. It's the plan working.

38
00:02:34,359 --> 00:02:36,691
Step four. Whatever's left gets one job.

39
00:02:37,111 --> 00:02:42,525
Paycheck 1: $665, minus $66, minus $320, minus $100.

40
00:02:42,945 --> 00:02:46,639
$179 left. Paycheck 2 leaves $178.

41
00:02:47,059 --> 00:02:52,011
That's about $357 a month, pointed at one target: the credit card.

42
00:02:52,431 --> 00:03:02,316
$3,200 at 24%. Paying a flat $96 minimum: 56 months and $2,126 in interest.

43
00:03:02,736 --> 00:03:07,673
Paying $453 a month: gone in 8 months, for $285 in interest.

44
00:03:08,093 --> 00:03:10,793
Same income. Same bills. A different plan.

45
00:03:11,213 --> 00:03:22,043
And here's a bonus. Paid every two weeks, you get twenty-six checks a year, so twice a year, a month has three paychecks. Your bills are already covered, so that whole check can go straight to your goal.

46
00:03:22,463 --> 00:03:23,325
So, recap.

47
00:03:23,485 --> 00:03:25,890
Give every bill a paycheck, and even them out.

48
00:03:26,050 --> 00:03:27,511
Turn surprises into a bill.

49
00:03:27,671 --> 00:03:28,797
Pick one fun number.

50
00:03:28,957 --> 00:03:30,666
And give the leftovers one job.

51
00:03:31,436 --> 00:03:39,397
The spreadsheet from this example is linked in the description. Put in your paychecks and bills, and it does the math, including your debt-free date.

52
00:03:39,817 --> 00:03:44,813
Subscribe, and next week we'll settle debt snowball versus avalanche, with real numbers.
